here as required.

What was learned from and ported verbatim from DeepSec
(rule-by-rule analysis in docs/02-deepsec-fidelity.md / .en.md):

- L1 rule set: secret regexes, sensitive-assignment + Shannon entropy
  analysis (thresholds 3.8/4.5, placeholder set, env-ref line rule),
  insecure-config and AI-pattern rules (`deepsec/shield/rules/patterns.py`)
- L2 SAST rules, 16 language-neutral regexes (`deepsec/shield/rules/sast.py`)
- L3 endpoint semantic checks and the LLM audit result normalization
  (`deepsec/shield/rules/ai_audit.py`)
- Scan orchestration: supported suffixes, skip lists, size limit, dedup
  semantics (`deepsec/shield/scanner.py`, `dedup.py`)
- The redaction-before-LLM principle and the audit JSON schema
- Quirks of the original regexes are preserved on purpose and pinned by
  tests so the two implementations stay comparable.

What is different here:

- The Python core, Python AST taint analysis and Tree-sitter WASM were NOT
  ported. L2/L3 semantic review is delegated to the agent CLI already
  installed on the user's machine (Claude / Codex / Gemini), with
  redaction applied first.
- agent_security and supply_chain modules were not ported
  (`seeds/known-packages.json` is an empty placeholder).
- The runtime is an Orca plugin (`pluginApi: 1`) instead of a VS Code
  extension: generated panel, terminal-routed actions, false-positive
  learning (`.vibeguard-learned.json`), content-fingerprint identities,
  and a four-locale UI (zh-TW / en / zh-CN / ja) are additions of this
  project.

